module Core

### A structured-concurrency scope: a named owner for a set of in-flight `Agent[T]`s whose lifetime
### is bound to this scope. Because a Nursery OWNS its children, they cannot outlive it — when the
### Nursery is torn down (its scope returns) each still-running child is ABANDONED, exactly as a bare
### `List[Agent[T]]` would be (a parked coroutine runs its cancellation shadow stack; a running worker
### thread is joined then discarded — a thread cannot be killed). That is the no-orphans guarantee,
### enforced structurally by ownership; no special teardown code is needed (the owned `agents` list is
### auto-destroyed, which abandons each child).
###
### Waiting for results is OPT-IN via `harvest!()` (the abandon-on-exit default is deliberate, not a
### Trio/Loom join-on-exit). Active cooperative cancellation is available via `cancel()`.
###
### Typical use:
###   var pool = Nursery[S64]()
###   pool.add(agent: compute(x: 30))      # adopt a suspended- or threaded-routine call result
###   pool.add(agent: worker(id: 1))
###   danger!
###     var rs = pool.harvest!()           # opt-in: wait for all, results in input order
###   return                               # (or just return: un-harvested children are abandoned)
entity Nursery[T]
  ### The children adopted into this scope, in adoption order. Owned: its auto-teardown abandons
  ### every still-running child (see the type doc), which is the whole no-orphans guarantee.
  secret agents: List[Agent[T]]

### Creates an empty scope with no children.
### :returns: a fresh `Nursery[T]`.
routine Nursery[T].$create() -> ?Nursery[T]
  var agents = List[Agent[T]]()
  return Nursery[T](agents: steal agents)

### Adopts an Agent into this scope, transferring ownership. Pass the result of a `suspended` or
### `threaded` routine call directly (a temporary needs no `steal`; a named binding does).
### :param agent: the Agent to adopt; ownership transfers into the scope.
routine Nursery[T].add(agent: Agent[T])
  me.agents.add_last(value: agent)
  return

### How many children this scope currently holds.
### :returns: the child count.
routine Nursery[T].count() -> U64
  return me.agents.count()

### Drives every child CONCURRENTLY and waits for ALL of them, returning their results in adoption
### order — the borrowing twin of `gather!` (which consumes a stolen list). Coroutine children
### progress on this thread's scheduler and thread children run on their OS threads, so the total
### wait is the SLOWEST child, not the sum. FAIL-FAST: if a child's routine throws, that throw
### propagates out and the remaining children are abandoned at scope teardown. The scope itself
### survives this call (children already completed here are freed later by the scope's teardown);
### each child is read as a temporary, so nothing aliases an Agent or double-frees.
### :returns: every child's value, in adoption order.
routine Nursery[T].harvest!() -> ?List[T]
  danger!
    var n = me.agents.count()
    var results = List[T]()
    var i = 0u64
    while i < n
      var value = me.agents[i].retrieve!()
      results.add_last(value: value)
      i = i + 1
    return results

### Requests cooperative cancellation of every child. This sets a flag (in native memory, reached
### through each child's handle) and returns immediately; it NEVER frees — teardown stays with the
### scope's `$destroy`. A child observes the request and stops on its own: a worker thread by polling
### `cancellation_requested()` (the only way to halt a thread — it cannot be killed), a coroutine the
### same way or by its next `waitfor` returning early. A child that ignores the request simply runs to
### completion and is joined/abandoned at teardown, as it would be anyway.
routine Nursery[T].cancel()
  danger!
    var n = me.agents.count()
    var i = 0u64
    while i < n
      me.agents[i].request_cancel()
      i = i + 1
  return
